error: output port `o` is driven, in a body whose every `clk.tick().await` is a bare statement and that crosses more than one clock edge per iteration, by a write whose timing the synthesized form cannot show. On that path the form is chosen by the write's operand, not by the reads before it: a value through a same-segment `let` wire is sampled at the segment's closing clock edge, while a register, an input or a constant is a continuous assign that shows the committed value at every observation. The simulator runs the write at the closing edge when an input read with a `clk.tick().await` after it precedes the write on the path, and at the segment's opening otherwise — so a wire written with no such read on some path shows one observation LATE in the hardware, and a register, input or constant written behind such a read shows one observation EARLY whenever its value can change at another edge (an input; a register assigned in another segment, or after the write; a constant in a segment after the first). Measured (2026-10-30, §10.7). Make the timing match the form: for a wire, read an input before every write of `o` in its segment; for a register, update it in the same segment before the write, or move the read past the `clk.tick().await`; or declare `o` as `RegOut<…>` (a registered output, immune because it commits at the clock edge). If this module exists to DEMONSTRATE the divergence, opt out explicitly with `#[hardware(sequential, allow_pretick_alignment)]`.
